<!-- Curriculum sub-strand: Core 1.2 -->

<chapter xml:id="ch-binomial-expansions" component="core" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Binomial Expansions</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      A binomial is an expression with two terms, such as <m>a + b</m> or <m>1 + 2x</m>. In earlier grades you expanded <m>(a + b)^2</m> by multiplying out the brackets, and in Grade <m>11</m> you met combinations.
    </p>
    <p>
      In this chapter you will expand binomial expressions up to the fourth power by multiplication and look for the pattern in the coefficients. You will then use that pattern to find the coefficients of terms in expansions up to the power of <m>10</m> without multiplying everything out, and use binomial expansions to evaluate numbers such as <m>(1.02)^{10}</m>.
    </p>
    <p>
      Binomial expansions give quick, accurate estimates where direct calculation is slow. They are used in working out compound growth, such as savings in a SACCO, and later in probability.
    </p>
  </introduction>

